.TH MAKI-ATTACH 8
.SH NAME
maki-attach \- manage the NBD, LVM and XFS attachment for a Maki volume
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B maki-attach attach
.B \-\-volume
.I name
.RB [ \-\-config
.IR file ]
.RI [ overrides ]
.RB [ \-\-init-sentinel ]
.RB [ \-\-plan ]
.br
.BR "maki-attach detach" | recover | cleanup
.B \-\-volume
.I name
.RB [ \-\-config
.IR file ]
.RI [ overrides ]
.RB [ \-\-plan ]
.br
.B maki-attach grow
.B \-\-volume
.I name
.B \-\-size-bytes
.I bytes
.RB [ \-\-config
.IR file ]
.RI [ overrides ]
.RB [ \-\-plan ]
.br
.B maki-attach verify
.B \-\-volume
.I name
.RB [ \-\-config
.IR file ]
.RB [ \-\-plan ]
.br
.B maki-attach recover-failed-start
.B \-\-volume
.I name
.RB [ \-\-plan ]
.br
.BR "maki-attach \-\-help" " | " \-\-version
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B attach
connects the NBD device and mounts the configured filesystem.
.B verify
checks the identity of the existing attachment.
.B grow
requires
.BI \-\-size-bytes " bytes"
and a verified, mounted attachment.
.PP
Stop workloads before running
.BR detach ", " recover " or " cleanup .
Use
.B recover
for a disconnected backend.
.B cleanup
uses the attachment record to select detach or recovery.
.B recover-failed-start
is the systemd failed-start cleanup hook. It queues the recovery coordinator
and, when the start died between LVM activation and the mapping proof, rolls
back that activation itself while the daemon still serves the device: the
scoped VG deactivation, the NBD disconnect, and the removal of the record. It
never unmounts.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BI \-\-volume " name"
The volume to act on. Every action requires it.
.TP
.BI \-\-config " file"
Read attachment settings from this file. The default path, when present, is
.IR /etc/maki/attach/name.toml .
.B recover-failed-start
refuses this option; to roll back an interrupted activation it reads the
default path, under the same ownership rule as the other actions.
.TP
.BR \-h ", " \-\-help
Print the usage and exit. Accepted only as the sole argument.
.TP
.BR \-V ", " \-\-version
Print the version and exit. Accepted only as the sole argument.
.TP
.B \-\-plan
Print the planned steps. Execution requires Linux and the volume identity.
.TP
.BI \-\-size-bytes " bytes"
Required by
.B grow
and refused by the other actions: the absolute minimum size of the logical
volume, in plain decimal digits and a multiple of 512.
.TP
.B \-\-init-sentinel
Accepted by
.B attach
only: create the volume sentinel on the mounted filesystem, as the setting
.B init_sentinel = true
does. The option cannot turn that setting off.
.SS Overrides
Each of these options replaces one attachment setting.
.BR attach ", " detach ", " recover ", " cleanup " and " grow
accept them;
.B verify
and
.B recover-failed-start
refuse them. The device block size and the LVM identity pins have no option
and come from the file only.
.TP
.BI \-\-nbd-device " device"
The NBD device
.RI ( /dev/nbdN ),
instead of the setting
.B nbd_device
or automatic allocation.
.TP
.BI \-\-socket " path"
The NBD socket of the daemon
.RB ( nbd_socket ).
.TP
.BI \-\-vg " name"
The volume group
.RB ( vg_name ).
.TP
.BI \-\-lv " name"
The logical volume
.RB ( lv_name ).
.TP
.BI \-\-mountpoint " directory"
The mountpoint
.RB ( mountpoint ).
.TP
.BI \-\-uuid " uuid"
The Maki volume UUID
.RB ( volume_uuid ).
.TP
.BI \-\-fs-uuid " uuid"
The expected XFS filesystem UUID
.RB ( fs_uuid ).
.SH EXIT STATUS
Zero indicates success. A failed action returns 1. Invalid command usage,
including a malformed option value, returns 2 and prints no plan. Without
Linux, an action run without
.B \-\-plan
returns 3.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR maki (8),
.BR maki-check (8)
